Build with the bundled llvm-mingw. Applied by installer/tools/assemble_payload.py to the payload's copy of the toolkit; the checked-out
repository is not edited.
clang for the MinGW target ignores __declspec(thread) ("unknown attribute 'thread' ignored"), so these per-thread variables became one
shared copy for all threads. The worst is spu_workload.c's per-thread job fingerprint cache: shared between the SPU worker threads, it
handed jobs the wrong image, every Spurs job chain waited 20 s and gave up, and the game never reached the title (Windows, installer
tests 2 and 5 of issue #57). _Thread_local is the same thing for MSVC and for clang/GCC on MinGW.
Tested on Windows 11 (v15 tree, bundled llvm-mingw): before, 0 frames and 20 "still closed" give-ups in 60 s; after, 509 flip lines
and 0 give-ups in 60 s, the menu at 36 s, a Free Battle at 29.5 flips/s and 0.0% silent sound blocks in the fight.
diff --git a/ps3recomp/libs/video/cellGcmSys.c b/ps3recomp/libs/video/cellGcmSys.c
--- a/ps3recomp/libs/video/cellGcmSys.c
+++ b/ps3recomp/libs/video/cellGcmSys.c
@@ -1877,7 +1877,7 @@ void cellGcm_fifo_kick_wait(unsigned ms)
              * so a kick ends the step at once. The spin was a third of this
              * thread's time, all of it kernel (jstars-notes CPU_PROFILE §26). */
             static int psleep = -1;
-            static __declspec(thread) HANDLE t_timer;
+            static _Thread_local HANDLE t_timer;
             if (psleep < 0) { const char* e = getenv("GCM_PUT_POLL_SLEEP"); psleep = (e && *e == '0') ? 0 : 1; }
             if (psleep && !t_timer)
                 t_timer = CreateWaitableTimerExW(NULL, NULL, 0x00000002 /* HIGH_RESOLUTION */, TIMER_ALL_ACCESS);
